{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":19,"query":"name=Kershaw&limit=10&title=Hitler","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":[{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"nonSort":{"@space":"preserve","#text":"The  "},"title":"\"Hitler myth\" ","subTitle":"image and reality in the Third Reich "},"name":[{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Kershaw, Ian","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Rawls, John,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1921-2002"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"former owner"}}}],"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"History"}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"enk"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"England"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Oxford "}},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York "}}],"issuance":"monographic","agent":[{"namePart":"Clarendon Press "},{"namePart":"Oxford University Press"}],"dateIssued":"1987"},"language":[{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},{"languageTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"}},{"languageTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"ger"}}],"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xii, 297 p. ; 22 cm."},"abstract":[{"@type":"Summary","#text":"Kershaw examines how the deification of 'the Fuhrer' and the Nazi's propaganda machine effectively solidified the Third Reich's hold on German culture from the 1920's to 1945."},{"@type":"Summary","#text":"\"Hitler's personality alone can scarcely explain his immense popularity and political effectiveness during the 1930s and '40s. Behind his strong hold over the German people lay the hopes and perceptions of the millions who adored him and consequently imbued him with larger-than-life characteristics. Based on secret popular opinion reports compiled by both the Nazis and their political enemies, this study of the Nazi state charts the creation, growth, and decline of the \"Hitler Myth.\" Kershaw demonstrates how the manufactured Fuhrer-cult formed a crucial integrating force in the Third Reich and acted as a vital catalyst in attaining Nazi political aims. Translated from German, this book affords readers a chilling look at how these masters of propaganda built on the beliefs, phobias, and predjudices of the day to create a popular image of Hitler that was at great odds with reality.\"--Publisher description."}],"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Introduction -- Part one : The making of the \"Hitler myth\", 1920-1940. Führer of the coming Germany : the Hitler image in the Weimar era -- Symbol of the nation : the propaganda profile of Hitler, 1933-1936 -- Führer without sin : Hitler and the \"little Hitlers\" -- The Führer versus the radicals : Hitler's image and the \"church struggle\" -- Hitler the statesman : war and peace in the balance -- Part two : The breaking of the \"Hitler myth\", 1940-1945. Blitzkrieg triumph : high peak of popularity, 1940-41 -- The war turns sour : the \"Hitler myth\" starts to crumble -- Defeat and disaster : the \"Hitler myth\" collapses -- Part three : The \"Hitler myth\" and the path to genocide. Hitler's popular image and the \"Jewish question\" -- Conclusion."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Ian Kershaw."},"Translation of: Der Hitler-Mythos.","Includes index.",{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Bibliography: p. [277]-287."}],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"e-gx---"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Hitler, Adolf,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1889-1945"}]}},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Public opinion","geographic":"Germany"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Propaganda, German","History"],"temporal":"20th century"},{"@authority":"lcsh","geographic":"Germany","topic":"Politics and government","temporal":"1933-1945"},{"@authority":"fast","temporal":"1900-1999"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"DD256.5 .K46513 1987"},{"@edition":"19","@authority":"ddc","#text":"943.086"}],"relatedItem":[{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Online version:","titleInfo":[{"title":"\"Hitler myth\""},{"@type":"uniform","title":"Hitler-Mythos. English"}],"originInfo":{"publisher":"Oxford : Clarendon Press ; New York : Oxford University Press, ©1987"},"identifier":{"@type":"local","#text":"(OCoLC)572510093"},"name":{"namePart":"Kershaw, Ian."}},{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Online version:","titleInfo":[{"title":"\"Hitler myth\""},{"@type":"uniform","title":"Hitler-Mythos. English"}],"originInfo":{"publisher":"Oxford : Clarendon Press ; New York : Oxford University Press, ©1987"},"identifier":{"@type":"local","#text":"(OCoLC)607749080"},"name":{"namePart":"Kershaw, Ian."}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990006460270203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0198219644 :"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^^86028583^"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"14819297"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no2014094889","#text":"Widener Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"DD256.5 .K46513 1987"},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/0000000460737207","#text":"Harvard University Archives"},"shelfLocator":"HUM 48.1 Box 4"},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/0000000463633307","#text":"Lamont Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"DD256.5 .K46513 1987"},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no2018017635","#text":"History Departmental Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"DD256.5 .K46513 1987"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":["History Dept.","Harvard University Archives","Lamont","Widener"]},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/990006460270203941","priorrecordids":{"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALEPH","#text":"000646027"}},"processingDate":"2025-10-13T05:14Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"descriptionStandard":"aacr","recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"DLC"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"861103"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20101022"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"990006460270203941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.8 using\n\t\t\t\t\tMARC21slim_MODS3-8_XSL2-0.xsl (Revision 2.79 2025/03/28)"}},{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"title":"Making friends with Hitler ","subTitle":"Lord Londonderry and Britain's road to war "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Kershaw, Ian","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"enk"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"England"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"London "}}],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Allen Lane"},"dateIssued":"2004"},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xix, 488 p., [16] p. of plates : ill., map ; 24 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Review","#text":"\"The arguments of Making Friends with Hitler are fundamental ones, reconfiguring the entire debate about appeasement and vividly re-creating a world in which the Nazis' ambitions remained genuinely hard to fathom. Kershaw, scornful though he is of British policy-making, concludes that even a government seeking to appease Hitler could not entertain Londonderry's wish to befriend him. But Londonderry's stance also compels us to look afresh at the other options available. If befriending Hitler and embracing his ferocious world view were out of the question for a democratic country with humane values, anxious to preserve peace, Kershaw shows how little possibility there was of removing, containing or deterring him. In this light, he argues, we should rethink our understanding of British action, the roles of Lord Londondrry, Neville Chamberlain and Winston Churchill, and the events that set Europe on the road to war.\"--Jacket."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Map : European territorial issues between the wars -- Prologue : a patrician's progress -- 1. Illusions and delusions about Hitler -- 2. Downfall of the air minister -- 3. Nazi friends -- 4. Lengthening shadows -- 5. Hope at last -- 6. End of the dream -- 7. Out in the cold -- Epilogue : Mount Stewart, September 1947."},"targetAudience":{"@authority":"marctarget","#text":"adult"},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Ian Kershaw."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references (p. 452-464) and index."}],"subject":[{"geographicCode":[{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"e-uk---"},{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"e-gx---"}]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["World War, 1939-1945","Diplomatic history"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":[{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"Marquess of"},"Londonderry, Charles Stewart Henry Vane-Tempest-Stewart,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1878-1949"}]}},{"@authority":"lcsh","geographic":"Great Britain","topic":"Foreign relations","temporal":"1936-1945"},{"@authority":"lcsh","geographic":["Great Britain","Germany"],"topic":"Foreign relations"},{"@authority":"lcsh","geographic":["Germany","Great Britain"],"topic":"Foreign relations"},{"@authority":"lcsh","geographic":"Germany","topic":"Foreign relations","temporal":"1933-1945"},{"temporal":"Geschichte 1933-1939"},{"@authority":"fast","temporal":"1933-1945"},{"@authority":"swd","temporal":"Geschichte 1933-1939"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"D750 .K49 2004b"},{"@edition":"22","@authority":"ddc","#text":"940.5311"}],"relatedItem":[{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Online version:","titleInfo":{"title":"Making friends with Hitler"},"originInfo":{"publisher":"London, England ; New York : Allen Lane, 2004"},"identifier":{"@type":"local","#text":"(OCoLC)645741411"},"name":{"namePart":"Kershaw, Ian."}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990095188840203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0713997176 (hbk.)"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"56646928"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no2014094889","#text":"Widener Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"D750 .K49 2004bx"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":"Widener"},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/990095188840203941","priorrecordids":{"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALEPH","#text":"009518884"}},"processingDate":"2025-12-09T06:40Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"descriptionStandard":"aacr","recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"UKM"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"040608"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20050208"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"990095188840203941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.8 using\n\t\t\t\t\tMARC21slim_MODS3-8_XSL2-0.xsl (Revision 2.79 2025/03/28)"}},{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"nonSort":{"@space":"preserve","#text":"Der  "},"title":"Hitler-Mythos ","subTitle":"Volksmeinung und Propaganda im Dritten Reich. Mit einer Einführung von Martin Broszat "},"name":[{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Kershaw, Ian,","role":[{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}},{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"author"}}]},{"@type":"personal","namePart":"Kershaw, Ian","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"marcrelator","#text":"aut"}}}],"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"dictionary"},{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"government publication"},"text"],"originInfo":[{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"gw"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"Germany"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso3166","#text":"DE"}}],"dateIssued":[{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2010"},"1980"],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"De Gruyter"},"edition":"1st ed."},{"@eventType":"publication","place":[{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Berlin ;"}},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Boston :"}}],"agent":[{"namePart":"Oldenbourg Wissenschaftsverlag,","role":{"roleTerm":"publisher"}},{"namePart":"Oldenbourg Wissenschaftsverlag,","role":{"roleTerm":"publisher"}}],"dateIssued":[2010]}],"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"ger"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"German"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":[{"@authority":"marccategory","#text":"electronic resource"},{"@authority":"marcsmd","#text":"remote"},{"@type":"media","@authority":"","#text":"computer"},{"@type":"carrier","@authority":"","#text":"online resource"}],"extent":"1 online resource (213 p.)"},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"Bei der historischen Deutung der nationalsozialistischen Epoche bildet die Zentralfigur Hitlers noch immer den Gegenstand kontroverser Erklärungen. Die Flut neuer Hitlerbiographien vermochte wenig beizutragen zur Klärung der Frage, wie es kommen konnte, dass ein Mann solchen persönlichen und ideologischen Zuschnitts von breiten Schichten des deutschen Volkes mit fast religiöser Inbrunst als Führer verehrt wurde. Die im engen Kontakt mit dem Institut für Zeitgeschichte entstandene Schrift des britischen Historikers Ian Kershaw ist wohl der erste systematische Versuch, nicht die Person Hitlers, sondern den sie umgebenden überdimensionalen Nimbus, von seiner Entstehung in der Weimarer Zeit bis zu seiner allmählichen Aufzehrung am Ende des Zweiten Weltkrieges, zu dokumentieren. Der Autor konnte sich dabei auf eine breite Überlieferung von Berichten über die Volksmeinung in der NS-Zeit stützen. Er verdeutlicht überzeugend, dass der hypertrophe Hitler-Mythos des Dritten Reiches nicht nur von einer perfektionierten Propaganda täglich neu erzeugt wurde, sondern in hohem Maße auch von naiven Volkserwartungen getragen und perfektioniert worden ist. Am Beispiel einzelner Ereigniskomplexe, z.B. die Röhm-Affäre 1934, wird exemplarisch veranschaulicht, wie weit sich die populäre Vorstellung von Hitler von dem wirklichen Denken und Handeln dieses Mannes entfernen konnte. Erst der zunehmend verselbständigte und introvertierte irrationale Führerglaube, hervorgegangen aus schweren nationalen und sozialen Krisen der deutschen Gesellschaft, vermittelte als Resonanzboden und soziale Integrationskraft des Dritten Reiches die von der Person Hitlers her allein unmöglich erklärbare enorme politische Wirkung des \"Führers\"."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Front Matter -- \"Der Führer des kommenden Deutschland\": Das Hitler-Bild in der Weimarer Zeit -- \"Sinnbild der Nation\": Die propagandistische Entfaltung des Führer-Mythos 1933-1936 -- Führer und Unterführer: Der Führer-Mythos als Kompensation für die Kritik an der Partei -- Im Schatten des Kirchenkampfes: Der Führer bremst die Radikalen -- Frieden oder Krieg: Die Phase der nationalen Hitler-Euphorie -- Der Verfall des Hitler-Mythos während des Krieges -- Wandel der Kriegslage 1942/43: Der Führer entrückt der Bevölkerung - Die Auflösung des Mythos setzt ein -- Endphase des Krieges und Zusammenbruch: Die Aufzehrung des Führer-Mythos -- Back Matter"},"targetAudience":{"@authority":"marctarget","#text":"specialized"},"accessCondition":{"@valueURI":"https://www.degruyter.com/dg/page/free-access-policy","@type":"useAndReproduction","#text":"This eBook is made available Open Access:"},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Ian Kershaw."},"Includes index.",{"@type":"additional physical form","#text":"Issued also in print."},{"@type":"language","#text":"German"},"Description based on online resource; title from PDF title page (publisher's Web site, viewed 15. Jun 2019)"],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"e-gx---"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Hitler, Adolf,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1889-1945"}]},"topic":"Public opinion"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Public opinion","geographic":"Germany"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Propaganda, German"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Political psychology"},{"@authority":"lcsh","geographic":"Germany","topic":["Politics and government","Public opinion"],"temporal":"1933-1945"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"DD256.5 .K477 1980"},{"@authority":"ddc","#text":"943.086"},{"@authority":"bisacsh","#text":"HIS000000 HIS037070"}],"relatedItem":[{"@type":"series","titleInfo":{"title":"Schriftenreihe der Vierteljahrshefte für Zeitgeschichte ","partNumber":"41"}},{"@type":"otherFormat","identifier":{"@type":"isbn","#text":"3-486-64541-2"}},{"@type":"series","titleInfo":{"title":"Schriftenreihe der Vierteljahrshefte für Zeitgeschichte ","partNumber":"Nummer 41"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/99153734211103941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"3-486-70339-0"},{"@type":"doi","#text":"10.1524/9783486703399"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"879550735"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"979853136"}],"location":[null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/99153734211103941","processingDate":"2026-10-01T04:14Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"descriptionStandard":"rda","recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"DE-B1597"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"190615"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20260928"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"99153734211103941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.8 using\n\t\t\t\t\tMARC21slim_MODS3-8_XSL2-0.xsl (Revision 2.79 2025/03/28)","languageOfCataloging":{"languageTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"}}}},{"titleInfo":{"title":"Hitler"},"name":{"@nameTitleGroup":"1","@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Kershaw, Ian","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"biography"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"History"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Biographies"}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"enk"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"England"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"London"}},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York"}}],"publisher":"Longman","dateIssued":"1991","issuance":"monographic"},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"viii, 230 p. ; 22 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"Adolf Hitler has left a lasting mark on the twentieth-century, as the dictator of Germany and instigator of a genocidal war, culminating in the ruin of much of Europe and the globe. This innovative best-seller explores the nature and mechanics of Hitler's power, and how he used it."},"tableOfContents":"Hitler's power: an enigma -- Power of the 'idea' -- Getting power -- Repression and power -- Plebiscitary power -- Expansion of power -- Absolute power -- Hubris of power -- Hitler's power and destruction.","note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Ian Kershaw."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references and index."}],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"e-gx---"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Hitler, Adolf",{"@type":"date","#text":"1889-1945"}]}},{"@authority":"fast","temporal":"1933-1945"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Heads of state","geographic":"Germany","genre":"Biography"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"National socialism"},{"@authority":"lcsh","geographic":"Germany","topic":"History","temporal":"1933-1945"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"DD247.H5 K46 1991"},{"@authority":"nlm","#text":"000084996"},{"@edition":"20","@authority":"ddc","#text":"943.086/092 B"}],"relatedItem":[{"titleInfo":{"title":"Profiles in power"}},{"@type":"series","titleInfo":{"title":"Profiles in power (London, England)"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990021631500203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0582080533 (Cased)"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"058255277X (paper)"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^^90013315^"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"22273338"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/0000000463633307","#text":"Lamont Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"DD247.H5 K46 1991"},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no2014094889","#text":"Widener Libr